      <floats-group id="article-22376.s17">
        <fig id="article-22376.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Hand Radiograph of Chronic Gout. Plain radiograph of the left hand showing characteristic findings of chronic topahgeous gout. There are multiple well-defined "punched-out" periarticular erosions with overhanging edges and adjacent soft tissue swelling consistent with tophus formation, most prominently involving the proximal interphalangeal joints. These radiographic features reflect long-standing monosodium urate crystal deposition and chronic inflammatory joint damage.&#x000a0; Contributed by S Dulebohn, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="gouty__destruction" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22376.s18">
        <fig id="article-22376.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Acute Gout Involving the First Metatarsophalangeal Joint. Clinical photograph showing erythema, swelling, and tenderness of the first Metatarsophalangeal joint, a classic presentation of acute gout known as podagra.&#x000a0;The inflamed joint and adjacent soft-tissue swelling reflect an acute inflammatory response triggered by the deposition of monosodium urate crystals. Podagra is the most common initial manifestation of gout and is often associated with the sudden onset of severe pain, warmth, and localized inflammation. Contributed by Steve Bhmji, MS, MD, PhD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="gout" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22376.s19">
        <fig id="article-22376.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Auricular Tophus in Chronic Gout. Clinical photograph demonstrating a tophus involving the helix of the ear. Auricular tophi appear as nodular swellings and reflect long-standing hyperuricemia with chronic crystal deposition.&#x000a0; Image courtesy S Bhimji MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="eargout" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22376.s20">
        <fig id="article-22376.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Monosodium Urate Crystals and Acute Gouty Inflammation. Needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals seen under polarized light microscopy (left) with associated inflammation of the first metatarsophalangeal joint (right), a classic presentation of acute gout.&#x000a0; Image courtesy O.Chaigasame</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="gouta" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22376.s21">
        <fig id="article-22376.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Tophaceous Gout Affecting the Hands. Clinical photograph showing multiple tophi involving the fingers, representing chronic gout with subcutaneous deposits of monosodium urate crystals.&#x000a0; Contributed by Dr. Shyam Verma, MBBS, DVD, FRCP, FAAD, Vadodara, India</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="gout__tophi__SV" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
